<exercises xml:id="exercises-inverses" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises</title>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-identity-entries-pattern">
	<title>Entry pattern in identity matrices</title>

	<p>
	As usual, let <m>I</m> represent the <m>n \times n</m> <xref ref="definition-inverses-identity-matrix" text="title" />.
	Describe the pattern of the entries of <m>I</m> in terms of the entry index pair <m>(i,j)</m>.
	That is,
	what is the <m>\nth{(i,j)}</m> entry of <m>I</m>,
	and how does the value of that entry depend on the indices <m>i</m> and <m>j</m>?
	</p>

	<!-- TODO valign="top" -->
	<aside valign="middle"><title>Further investigation</title><p>
		After you are satisfied with your response to <xref ref="exercise-inverses-identity-entries-pattern" text="type-local" />,
		search the internet for an explanation of the <term>Kronecker delta symbol</term>.
	</p></aside>

	<p><alert>Note:</alert>
	your response should be a description in words,
	not a formula in <m>i</m> and <m>j</m>.
	</p>

</exercise>

